[2026-02-04T17:38:00.338Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019dJu8UQ54NcAGjQJyG6bGd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Trump administration denaturalization 200 per month quota 2026 legal authority\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Trump administration wants to set quota for denaturalizing American citizens : NPR\",\"url\":\"https://www.npr.org/2025/12/24/nx-s1-5649729/trump-administration-wants-to-set-quota-for-denaturalizing-american-citizens\"},{\"title\":\"Trump Administration Sets Goal to Denaturalize Thousands of US Citizens in 2026 | Truthout\",\"url\":\"https://truthout.org/articles/trump-administration-sets-goal-to-denaturalize-thousands-of-us-citizens-in-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump Denaturalization Quota: 5 Essential Facts You Need\",\"url\":\"https://www.lawfirm4immigrants.com/trump-denaturalization-quota/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump Administration Plans Historic Expansion of Denaturalization Efforts\",\"url\":\"https://berardiimmigrationlaw.com/trump-administration-plans-historic-expansion-of-denaturalization-efforts/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump ramps up denaturalization push with 2026 quota targeting naturalized citizens | NationofChange\",\"url\":\"https://www.nationofchange.org/2025/12/19/trump-ramps-up-denaturalization-push-with-2026-quota-targeting-naturalized-citizens/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump Threatens to Denaturalize U.S. Citizens If They 'Deserve' It | TIME\",\"url\":\"https://time.com/7344931/trump-threatens-to-denaturalize-us-citizens/\"},{\"title\":\"'Straight-Up Nazi Stuff': Trump Admin Plans to Strip More Naturalized Americans of Citizenship | Common Dreams\",\"url\":\"https://www.commondreams.org/news/trump-deporting-naturalized-citizens\"},{\"title\":\"Trump administration seeks sharp increase in denaturalization cases, NYT reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/americas/trump-administration-seeks-sharp-increase-in-denaturalization-cases-nyt-reports/3774448\"},{\"title\":\"Trump administration sets goal to denaturalize thousands of U.S. citizens in 2026 | East County Magazine\",\"url\":\"https://www.eastcountymagazine.org/trump-administration-sets-goal-denaturalize-thousands-us-citizens-2026\"},{\"title\":\"Justice Department eyes hundreds of new denaturalization cases targeting convicts, war criminals | Washington Times\",\"url\":\"https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2026/feb/3/justice-department-eyes-hundreds-new-denaturalization-cases-targeting/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the Trump administration's denaturalization quota and legal authority:\n\n## The Quota\n\nThe administration says it wants to denaturalize 100 to 200 people per month in 2026. USCIS should work with the Department of Justice to meet that quota.\n\n## Historical Context\n\nBetween 1990 and 2017, the United States averaged approximately 11 denaturalization cases per year. During Trump's first administration, about 170 denaturalization cases were sent to court.\n\n## Legal Authority\n\nUnder federal law, denaturalization is permitted primarily when citizenship was obtained through fraud, willful misrepresentation of material facts, or concealment of relevant information. Additionally, only federal courts have the authority to denaturalize a U.S. citizen, and the government must meet a high evidentiary standard.\n\n## Legal and Practical Concerns\n\nCritics and legal experts have raised significant concerns about the quota approach. Sarah Pierce, a former USCIS official, stated that requiring monthly quotas \"10 times higher than the total annual number of denaturalizations in recent years turns a serious and rare tool into a blunt instrument and fuels unnecessary fear and uncertainty for the millions of naturalized Americans.\" \n\nFormer government officials and legal experts have questioned whether the administration's stated goal is even feasible within existing legal and institutional constraints, with a former Department of Homeland Security official noting it would be \"virtually impossible\" for agencies to process the volume of cases demanded by the guidance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"ef43bbbb-9ebb-4e23-8f25-ed7b3792cc47","tool_use_result":{"query":"Trump administration denaturalization 200 per month quota 2026 legal authority","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GemtR6Q6awFSLbX4eFjEBc","content":[{"title":"Trump administration wants to set quota for denaturalizing American citizens : NPR","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/12/24/nx-s1-5649729/trump-administration-wants-to-set-quota-for-denaturalizing-american-citizens"},{"title":"Trump Administration Sets Goal to Denaturalize Thousands of US Citizens in 2026 | Truthout","url":"https://truthout.org/articles/trump-administration-sets-goal-to-denaturalize-thousands-of-us-citizens-in-2026/"},{"title":"Trump Denaturalization Quota: 5 Essential Facts You Need","url":"https://www.lawfirm4immigrants.com/trump-denaturalization-quota/"},{"title":"Trump Administration Plans Historic Expansion of Denaturalization Efforts","url":"https://berardiimmigrationlaw.com/trump-administration-plans-historic-expansion-of-denaturalization-efforts/"},{"title":"Trump ramps up denaturalization push with 2026 quota targeting naturalized citizens | NationofChange","url":"https://www.nationofchange.org/2025/12/19/trump-ramps-up-denaturalization-push-with-2026-quota-targeting-naturalized-citizens/"},{"title":"Trump Threatens to Denaturalize U.S. Citizens If They 'Deserve' It | TIME","url":"https://time.com/7344931/trump-threatens-to-denaturalize-us-citizens/"},{"title":"'Straight-Up Nazi Stuff': Trump Admin Plans to Strip More Naturalized Americans of Citizenship | Common Dreams","url":"https://www.commondreams.org/news/trump-deporting-naturalized-citizens"},{"title":"Trump administration seeks sharp increase in denaturalization cases, NYT reports","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/americas/trump-administration-seeks-sharp-increase-in-denaturalization-cases-nyt-reports/3774448"},{"title":"Trump administration sets goal to denaturalize thousands of U.S. citizens in 2026 | East County Magazine","url":"https://www.eastcountymagazine.org/trump-administration-sets-goal-denaturalize-thousands-us-citizens-2026"},{"title":"Justice Department eyes hundreds of new denaturalization cases targeting convicts, war criminals | Washington Times","url":"https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2026/feb/3/justice-department-eyes-hundreds-new-denaturalization-cases-targeting/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about the Trump administration's denaturalization quota and legal authority:\n\n## The Quota\n\nThe administration says it wants to denaturalize 100 to 200 people per month in 2026. USCIS should work with the Department of Justice to meet that quota.\n\n## Historical Context\n\nBetween 1990 and 2017, the United States averaged approximately 11 denaturalization cases per year. During Trump's first administration, about 170 denaturalization cases were sent to court.\n\n## Legal Authority\n\nUnder federal law, denaturalization is permitted primarily when citizenship was obtained through fraud, willful misrepresentation of material facts, or concealment of relevant information. Additionally, only federal courts have the authority to denaturalize a U.S. citizen, and the government must meet a high evidentiary standard.\n\n## Legal and Practical Concerns\n\nCritics and legal experts have raised significant concerns about the quota approach. Sarah Pierce, a former USCIS official, stated that requiring monthly quotas \"10 times higher than the total annual number of denaturalizations in recent years turns a serious and rare tool into a blunt instrument and fuels unnecessary fear and uncertainty for the millions of naturalized Americans.\" \n\nFormer government officials and legal experts have questioned whether the administration's stated goal is even feasible within existing legal and institutional constraints, with a former Department of Homeland Security official noting it would be \"virtually impossible\" for agencies to process the volume of cases demanded by the guidance."],"durationSeconds":7.373332699999999}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:01.411Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MQZPRNCjxubxspnMuLCP86","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"denaturalization process due process protections constitutional rights\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Denaturalization Under the Trump Administration: Legal Standards, Constitutional Constraints, and DOJ Enforcement Priorities - Law Offices of Michael D. BakerLaw Offices of Michael D. Baker\",\"url\":\"https://mikebakerlaw.com/blog/2025/07/02/denaturalization-trump-administration-legal-standards-constitutional-constraints-doj-enforcement/\"},{\"title\":\"Denaturalization Defense: Protecting Your U.S. Citizenship Rights\",\"url\":\"https://www.stevelopezlaw.com/understanding-denaturalization-what-you-need-to-know-if-your-citizenship-is-at-risk\"},{\"title\":\"DENATURALIZATION AND REVOCATION OF ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ilrc.org/sites/default/files/resources/denaturalization_pa.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Early Denaturalization Jurisprudence | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress\",\"url\":\"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C4-1-5-2/ALDE_00013171/\"},{\"title\":\"The Denaturalization Consequences of Guilty Pleas | Yale Law Journal\",\"url\":\"https://yalelawjournal.org/forum/the-denaturalization-consequences-of-guilty-pleas\"},{\"title\":\"Aliens in the United States | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress\",\"url\":\"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C18-8-7-2/ALDE_00001262/\"},{\"title\":\"What You Need to Know About Denaturalization in the U.S.\",\"url\":\"https://rjimmigrationlaw.com/resources/what-you-need-to-know-about-denaturalization-in-the-u-s/\"},{\"title\":\"Denaturalization (Revoking Citizenship) Generally | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress\",\"url\":\"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C4-1-5-1/ALDE_00013170/\"},{\"title\":\"How the Supreme Court Rejected Denaturalization as a Political Weapon Long Ago | Brennan Center for Justice\",\"url\":\"https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/supreme-court-rejected-denaturalization-political-weapon-long-ago\"},{\"title\":\"The Rising Threat of Denaturalization: What Every Naturalized Citizen Should Know - Francis Law Immigration\",\"url\":\"https://francislawimmigration.com/the-rising-threat-of-denaturalization-what-every-naturalized-citizen-should-know/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about denaturalization, due process protections, and constitutional rights:\n\n## Key Constitutional Protections\n\nUnder the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause, the government must prove that a citizen specifically intended to surrender their U.S. citizenship, not merely that they voluntarily committed an expatriating act. Additionally, under the Fourteenth Amendment, U.S. citizenship cannot be stripped without due process.\n\n## Due Process Standards\n\nThe standard of proof in denaturalization proceedings is deliberately high: the government must show \"clear, unequivocal, and convincing evidence\" in civil cases, or \"beyond a reasonable doubt\" in criminal proceedings. Meaningful judicial review requirements and due process protections create significant obstacles to mass denaturalization efforts.\n\n## Judicial Review Requirement\n\nOnly a U.S. District Judge has the authority to issue a judgment that revokes a person's naturalized citizenship, and this judicial safeguard ensures that citizenship cannot be arbitrarily taken away by executive agencies.\n\n## Limitations on Civil Denaturalization\n\nHowever, there are significant due process concerns in civil proceedings. Denaturalization cases are usually civil—not criminal—meaning there is no guaranteed right to an attorney, no right to a public hearing, and no requirement to notify the individual before a case is filed. In some cases, naturalized citizens may lose their citizenship without ever being given a chance to defend themselves.\n\n## Legal Grounds\n\nThe Immigration and Nationality Act (INA) only permits denaturalization in limited cases, such as willful misrepresentation or concealment of a material fact during the naturalization process, or involvement in subversive groups within five years of naturalization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"2f5ee4cf-aa4c-4c47-9401-63d538c65bc8","tool_use_result":{"query":"denaturalization process due process protections constitutional rights","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FazKC3FwVTA75pwhEsSbny","content":[{"title":"Denaturalization Under the Trump Administration: Legal Standards, Constitutional Constraints, and DOJ Enforcement Priorities - Law Offices of Michael D. BakerLaw Offices of Michael D. Baker","url":"https://mikebakerlaw.com/blog/2025/07/02/denaturalization-trump-administration-legal-standards-constitutional-constraints-doj-enforcement/"},{"title":"Denaturalization Defense: Protecting Your U.S. Citizenship Rights","url":"https://www.stevelopezlaw.com/understanding-denaturalization-what-you-need-to-know-if-your-citizenship-is-at-risk"},{"title":"DENATURALIZATION AND REVOCATION OF ...","url":"https://www.ilrc.org/sites/default/files/resources/denaturalization_pa.pdf"},{"title":"Early Denaturalization Jurisprudence | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C4-1-5-2/ALDE_00013171/"},{"title":"The Denaturalization Consequences of Guilty Pleas | Yale Law Journal","url":"https://yalelawjournal.org/forum/the-denaturalization-consequences-of-guilty-pleas"},{"title":"Aliens in the United States | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C18-8-7-2/ALDE_00001262/"},{"title":"What You Need to Know About Denaturalization in the U.S.","url":"https://rjimmigrationlaw.com/resources/what-you-need-to-know-about-denaturalization-in-the-u-s/"},{"title":"Denaturalization (Revoking Citizenship) Generally | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C4-1-5-1/ALDE_00013170/"},{"title":"How the Supreme Court Rejected Denaturalization as a Political Weapon Long Ago | Brennan Center for Justice","url":"https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/supreme-court-rejected-denaturalization-political-weapon-long-ago"},{"title":"The Rising Threat of Denaturalization: What Every Naturalized Citizen Should Know - Francis Law Immigration","url":"https://francislawimmigration.com/the-rising-threat-of-denaturalization-what-every-naturalized-citizen-should-know/"}]},"Based on the web search results, here's what I found about denaturalization, due process protections, and constitutional rights:\n\n## Key Constitutional Protections\n\nUnder the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause, the government must prove that a citizen specifically intended to surrender their U.S. citizenship, not merely that they voluntarily committed an expatriating act. Additionally, under the Fourteenth Amendment, U.S. citizenship cannot be stripped without due process.\n\n## Due Process Standards\n\nThe standard of proof in denaturalization proceedings is deliberately high: the government must show \"clear, unequivocal, and convincing evidence\" in civil cases, or \"beyond a reasonable doubt\" in criminal proceedings. Meaningful judicial review requirements and due process protections create significant obstacles to mass denaturalization efforts.\n\n## Judicial Review Requirement\n\nOnly a U.S. District Judge has the authority to issue a judgment that revokes a person's naturalized citizenship, and this judicial safeguard ensures that citizenship cannot be arbitrarily taken away by executive agencies.\n\n## Limitations on Civil Denaturalization\n\nHowever, there are significant due process concerns in civil proceedings. Denaturalization cases are usually civil—not criminal—meaning there is no guaranteed right to an attorney, no right to a public hearing, and no requirement to notify the individual before a case is filed. In some cases, naturalized citizens may lose their citizenship without ever being given a chance to defend themselves.\n\n## Legal Grounds\n\nThe Immigration and Nationality Act (INA) only permits denaturalization in limited cases, such as willful misrepresentation or concealment of a material fact during the naturalization process, or involvement in subversive groups within five years of naturalization."],"durationSeconds":7.791317000000001}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:02.053Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MVNhq2DUUXgRMSWf1gULYb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"8 USC 1451 denaturalization statute legal authority immigration\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"8 U.S. Code § 1451 - Revocation of naturalization | U.S. Code | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/8/1451\"},{\"title\":\"REVOCATION OF CITIZENSHIP\",\"url\":\"https://biotech.law.lsu.edu/blaw/olc/ina340.htm\"},{\"title\":\"8 U.S.C. § 1451 - U.S. Code Title 8. Aliens and Nationality § 1451 | FindLaw\",\"url\":\"https://codes.findlaw.com/us/title-8-aliens-and-nationality/8-usc-sect-1451/\"},{\"title\":\"Early Denaturalization Jurisprudence | U.S. Constitution Annotated | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-8/clause-4/early-denaturalization-jurisprudence\"},{\"title\":\"\\\"Snap: How the Moral Elasticity of the Denaturalization Statute Goes To\\\" by Aram A. Gavoor and Daniel Miktus\",\"url\":\"https://scholarship.law.wm.edu/wmborj/vol23/iss3/3/\"},{\"title\":\"Concealing Material Facts When Procuring Citizenship | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress\",\"url\":\"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C4-1-5-5/ALDE_00013174/\"},{\"title\":\"Office of Public Affairs | The Department of Justice Creates Section Dedicated to Denaturalization Cases | United States Department of Justice\",\"url\":\"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/department-justice-creates-section-dedicated-denaturalization-cases\"},{\"title\":\"Page 519 TITLE 8—ALIENS AND NATIONALITY § 1451\",\"url\":\"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/8/1451\"},{\"title\":\"Grounds for Denaturalization (Revocation of U.S. Citizenship) | Oberheiden P.C\",\"url\":\"https://federal-lawyer.com/criminal-law/denaturalization-attorney/reasons-for-us-citizenship-revocation/\"},{\"title\":\"Denaturalization Investigations Handbook\",\"url\":\"https://www.ice.gov/doclib/foia/policy/handbook_HSI_08-01_DenaturalizationInv_01.15.2008.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's comprehensive information about 8 USC 1451 and denaturalization:\n\n## Overview\n\n8 U.S.C. § 1451(a) is the controlling statute for denaturalization proceedings in the Immigration and Nationality Act, which provides that U.S. attorneys must institute proceedings in district courts to revoke citizenship and cancel naturalization certificates on the ground that such orders and certificates were illegally procured or were procured by concealment of a material fact or by willful misrepresentation.\n\n## Legal Grounds for Denaturalization\n\nSection 340(a) (8 U.S.C. § 1451) provides two distinct legal bases for denaturalization: first, the government may seek revocation if the naturalized person has procured citizenship illegally; and second, revocation is available if the person procured naturalization \"by concealment of a material fact or by willful misrepresentation.\"\n\n## Procedural Authority\n\nThe Department of Justice has created a Denaturalization Section dedicated to investigating and litigating revocation of naturalization within the Civil Division's Office of Immigration Litigation.\n\n## Time Limitations\n\nNo statute of limitations applies to a judicial denaturalization proceeding under section 340(a). Civil denaturalization cases have no statute of limitations, and the Department has successfully denaturalized individuals who have illegally obtained citizenship, including terrorists and other national security threats, war criminals, human rights violators, sex offenders, and other fraudsters.\n\n## Related Statutory Provisions\n\nSection 1451(c) provides that if a person naturalized after December 24, 1952 becomes a member of or affiliated with an organization within five years following naturalization where such membership would have precluded naturalization, this constitutes prima facie evidence of ineligibility at the time of naturalization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"d1fe53d8-4773-4b42-bb12-8c1dac79d865","tool_use_result":{"query":"8 USC 1451 denaturalization statute legal authority immigration","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GUCbiAcaDkkdx4iiwhVKk2","content":[{"title":"8 U.S. Code § 1451 - Revocation of naturalization | U.S. Code | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/8/1451"},{"title":"REVOCATION OF CITIZENSHIP","url":"https://biotech.law.lsu.edu/blaw/olc/ina340.htm"},{"title":"8 U.S.C. § 1451 - U.S. Code Title 8. Aliens and Nationality § 1451 | FindLaw","url":"https://codes.findlaw.com/us/title-8-aliens-and-nationality/8-usc-sect-1451/"},{"title":"Early Denaturalization Jurisprudence | U.S. Constitution Annotated | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-8/clause-4/early-denaturalization-jurisprudence"},{"title":"\"Snap: How the Moral Elasticity of the Denaturalization Statute Goes To\" by Aram A. Gavoor and Daniel Miktus","url":"https://scholarship.law.wm.edu/wmborj/vol23/iss3/3/"},{"title":"Concealing Material Facts When Procuring Citizenship | Constitution Annotated | Congress.gov | Library of Congress","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/artI-S8-C4-1-5-5/ALDE_00013174/"},{"title":"Office of Public Affairs | The Department of Justice Creates Section Dedicated to Denaturalization Cases | United States Department of Justice","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/department-justice-creates-section-dedicated-denaturalization-cases"},{"title":"Page 519 TITLE 8—ALIENS AND NATIONALITY § 1451","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/8/1451"},{"title":"Grounds for Denaturalization (Revocation of U.S. Citizenship) | Oberheiden P.C","url":"https://federal-lawyer.com/criminal-law/denaturalization-attorney/reasons-for-us-citizenship-revocation/"},{"title":"Denaturalization Investigations Handbook","url":"https://www.ice.gov/doclib/foia/policy/handbook_HSI_08-01_DenaturalizationInv_01.15.2008.pdf"}]},"Based on the search results, here's comprehensive information about 8 USC 1451 and denaturalization:\n\n## Overview\n\n8 U.S.C. § 1451(a) is the controlling statute for denaturalization proceedings in the Immigration and Nationality Act, which provides that U.S. attorneys must institute proceedings in district courts to revoke citizenship and cancel naturalization certificates on the ground that such orders and certificates were illegally procured or were procured by concealment of a material fact or by willful misrepresentation.\n\n## Legal Grounds for Denaturalization\n\nSection 340(a) (8 U.S.C. § 1451) provides two distinct legal bases for denaturalization: first, the government may seek revocation if the naturalized person has procured citizenship illegally; and second, revocation is available if the person procured naturalization \"by concealment of a material fact or by willful misrepresentation.\"\n\n## Procedural Authority\n\nThe Department of Justice has created a Denaturalization Section dedicated to investigating and litigating revocation of naturalization within the Civil Division's Office of Immigration Litigation.\n\n## Time Limitations\n\nNo statute of limitations applies to a judicial denaturalization proceeding under section 340(a). Civil denaturalization cases have no statute of limitations, and the Department has successfully denaturalized individuals who have illegally obtained citizenship, including terrorists and other national security threats, war criminals, human rights violators, sex offenders, and other fraudsters.\n\n## Related Statutory Provisions\n\nSection 1451(c) provides that if a person naturalized after December 24, 1952 becomes a member of or affiliated with an organization within five years following naturalization where such membership would have precluded naturalization, this constitutes prima facie evidence of ineligibility at the time of naturalization."],"durationSeconds":7.9139033}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:03.681Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PUprwWhUBZBMAZExRdQsWw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ICE detention population 2025 2026 record high statistics\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ICE's detainee population reaches new record high of 73,000, as crackdown widens - CBS News\",\"url\":\"https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ices-detainee-population-record-high-of-73000/\"},{\"title\":\"92% of ICE Detention Growth in FY 2026 Driven by Immigrants with No Criminal Convictions\",\"url\":\"https://austinkocher.substack.com/p/92-of-ice-detention-growth-in-fy\"},{\"title\":\"New Report Details ICE's Expanding and Increasingly ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/blog/ice-expanding-detention-system/\"},{\"title\":\"Show Your Work: The Math Behind My Claim that 92% of ICE's Detention Growth Comes from People with No Criminal Convictions\",\"url\":\"https://austinkocher.substack.com/p/show-your-work-the-math-behind-my\"},{\"title\":\"Immigration Detention Quick Facts\",\"url\":\"https://tracreports.org/immigration/quickfacts/\"},{\"title\":\"ICE's Delayed Detention Data is Out, Two Record Highs: 70,000 People Detained, 40,000 on Ankle Monitors\",\"url\":\"https://austinkocher.substack.com/p/ices-delayed-detention-data-is-out\"},{\"title\":\"ICE Detainees Statistics in US 2026 | Key Facts - The World Data\",\"url\":\"https://theworlddata.com/ice-detainees-statistics-in-us/\"},{\"title\":\"New ICE arrest data show the power of state and local governments to curtail mass deportations\",\"url\":\"https://www.prisonpolicy.org/blog/2025/12/11/ice-jails-update/\"},{\"title\":\"How many people did ICE detain in 2025 and how do dete...\",\"url\":\"https://factually.co/fact-checks/justice/ice-detentions-2025-numbers-and-trends-compare-to-previous-years-3ee367\"},{\"title\":\"Chart: ICE Is Expanding Its Detention Network | Statista\",\"url\":\"https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/35773/ice-detention-centers-in-the-united-states/?__sso_cookie_checker=failed\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key ICE detention population statistics for 2025-2026:\n\n## Record High Detention Numbers\n\nAs of mid-January 2026, ICE was holding about 73,000 individuals facing deportation in its custody across the country, the highest level recorded by the agency and an 84% increase from the same time in 2025, when its detention population hovered below 40,000.\n\nMore recent data shows continued growth: On January 24, 2026, 70,766 people were held in 225 immigrant detention facilities across the country, and this number represents the highest detained population on public record and the first time that the detained population has crossed 70,000.\n\n## Composition of Detainees\n\nA significant portion of the detained population lacks criminal convictions. The growth in the detained population of people arrested by ICE since the end of the fiscal year in September has come almost exclusively from the growth in numbers of people detained with no criminal charges or convictions. Between September 21, 2025, and January 7, 2026, single-day ICE detentions increased 11,296, but only 902 of those were convicted criminals, 2,273 had pending criminal charges and 8,121 were other immigrant violators.\n\n## Expansion of Infrastructure\n\nThe Trump administration has said it is aiming to be able to detain upwards of 100,000 immigration detainees at any given time, as part of its government-wide effort to carry out a deportation crackdown of unprecedented proportions. The steady growth in ICE's detainee population over the past months comes after the agency received an unprecedented infusion of funding through the One Big Beautiful Act last year, including $45 billion alone to expand detention space.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"4f3376ea-cea6-4e4d-904f-77adbb67ff84","tool_use_result":{"query":"ICE detention population 2025 2026 record high statistics","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01SceTDVy7kkXHFTPJ5Gci4R","content":[{"title":"ICE's detainee population reaches new record high of 73,000, as crackdown widens - CBS News","url":"https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ices-detainee-population-record-high-of-73000/"},{"title":"92% of ICE Detention Growth in FY 2026 Driven by Immigrants with No Criminal Convictions","url":"https://austinkocher.substack.com/p/92-of-ice-detention-growth-in-fy"},{"title":"New Report Details ICE's Expanding and Increasingly ...","url":"https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/blog/ice-expanding-detention-system/"},{"title":"Show Your Work: The Math Behind My Claim that 92% of ICE's Detention Growth Comes from People with No Criminal Convictions","url":"https://austinkocher.substack.com/p/show-your-work-the-math-behind-my"},{"title":"Immigration Detention Quick Facts","url":"https://tracreports.org/immigration/quickfacts/"},{"title":"ICE's Delayed Detention Data is Out, Two Record Highs: 70,000 People Detained, 40,000 on Ankle Monitors","url":"https://austinkocher.substack.com/p/ices-delayed-detention-data-is-out"},{"title":"ICE Detainees Statistics in US 2026 | Key Facts - The World Data","url":"https://theworlddata.com/ice-detainees-statistics-in-us/"},{"title":"New ICE arrest data show the power of state and local governments to curtail mass deportations","url":"https://www.prisonpolicy.org/blog/2025/12/11/ice-jails-update/"},{"title":"How many people did ICE detain in 2025 and how do dete...","url":"https://factually.co/fact-checks/justice/ice-detentions-2025-numbers-and-trends-compare-to-previous-years-3ee367"},{"title":"Chart: ICE Is Expanding Its Detention Network | Statista","url":"https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/35773/ice-detention-centers-in-the-united-states/?__sso_cookie_checker=failed"}]},"Based on the search results, here are the key ICE detention population statistics for 2025-2026:\n\n## Record High Detention Numbers\n\nAs of mid-January 2026, ICE was holding about 73,000 individuals facing deportation in its custody across the country, the highest level recorded by the agency and an 84% increase from the same time in 2025, when its detention population hovered below 40,000.\n\nMore recent data shows continued growth: On January 24, 2026, 70,766 people were held in 225 immigrant detention facilities across the country, and this number represents the highest detained population on public record and the first time that the detained population has crossed 70,000.\n\n## Composition of Detainees\n\nA significant portion of the detained population lacks criminal convictions. The growth in the detained population of people arrested by ICE since the end of the fiscal year in September has come almost exclusively from the growth in numbers of people detained with no criminal charges or convictions. Between September 21, 2025, and January 7, 2026, single-day ICE detentions increased 11,296, but only 902 of those were convicted criminals, 2,273 had pending criminal charges and 8,121 were other immigrant violators.\n\n## Expansion of Infrastructure\n\nThe Trump administration has said it is aiming to be able to detain upwards of 100,000 immigration detainees at any given time, as part of its government-wide effort to carry out a deportation crackdown of unprecedented proportions. The steady growth in ICE's detainee population over the past months comes after the agency received an unprecedented infusion of funding through the One Big Beautiful Act last year, including $45 billion alone to expand detention space."],"durationSeconds":8.760905500000002}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:04.133Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UeR4r4vTMsimVzSKizAgU2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"denaturalization task force USCIS cases pending legal challenges\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Denaturalization Task Force How Citizenship Can Be Revoked\",\"url\":\"https://www.shusterman.com/denaturalization/\"},{\"title\":\"Featured Issue: Denaturalization\",\"url\":\"https://www.aila.org/featured-issue-denaturalization\"},{\"title\":\"FAQs: How Denaturalization Works | Immigrant Legal Resource Center | ILRC\",\"url\":\"https://www.ilrc.org/community-resources/faqs-how-denaturalization-works\"},{\"title\":\"Featured Issue: Denaturalization Efforts by USCIS\",\"url\":\"https://www.aila.org/library/featured-issue-denaturalization-efforts-by-uscis\"},{\"title\":\"Office of Public Affairs | The Department of Justice Creates Section Dedicated to Denaturalization Cases | United States Department of Justice\",\"url\":\"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/department-justice-creates-section-dedicated-denaturalization-cases\"},{\"title\":\"Office of the Assistant Attorney General U.S. Department of Justice\",\"url\":\"https://www.justice.gov/civil/media/1404046/dl?inline=\"},{\"title\":\"Denaturalization Under the Trump Administration: Legal Standards, Constitutional Constraints, and DOJ Enforcement Priorities - Law Offices of Michael D. BakerLaw Offices of Michael D. Baker\",\"url\":\"https://mikebakerlaw.com/blog/2025/07/02/denaturalization-trump-administration-legal-standards-constitutional-constraints-doj-enforcement/\"},{\"title\":\"USCIS partners with Justice Department and Secures First Denaturalization As a Result of Operation Janus | USCIS\",\"url\":\"https://www.uscis.gov/archive/uscis-partners-with-justice-department-and-secures-first-denaturalization-as-a-result-of-operation\"},{\"title\":\"Reported: USCIS ordered to escalate denaturalization case referrals | Immigration Policy Tracking Project\",\"url\":\"https://immpolicytracking.org/policies/uscis-reportedly-set-to-escalate-denaturalization-case-referrals/\"},{\"title\":\"Can Trump Take Away My Citizenship: Denaturalization in 2025\",\"url\":\"https://www.lawfirm4immigrants.com/can-trump-take-away-my-citizenship/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the denaturalization task force, USCIS cases, and legal challenges:\n\n## USCIS Denaturalization Task Force and Case Volume\n\nIn December 2025, USCIS asked its field offices to \"supply Office of Immigration Litigation with 100-200 denaturalization cases per month\". By comparison, just over 120 cases have been filed between 2017 and 2025; this year, the DOJ has brought 13 denaturalization cases and won 8.\n\nThe USCIS has established an office in Los Angeles to focus on denaturalization. It will refer potential cases to the US Department of Justice.\n\n## Pending Cases and Legal Challenges\n\nSeveral legal and constitutional challenges exist to denaturalization efforts:\n\nIn Maslenjak v. United States (2017), the Supreme Court unanimously ruled that a naturalized citizen cannot lose their citizenship for a lie or omission unless the government proves that the falsehood was \"material\".\n\nAt this point in time, revocation of naturalization can only occur in federal courts. USCIS can only cancel the certification of naturalization, but cannot revoke the underlying status.\n\nThe 2025 Executive Order 14160 sought to redefine birthright citizenship, but it cannot retroactively revoke citizenship already granted under the 14th Amendment. The order's implementation is being challenged in court, and federal judges have blocked its application pending review.\n\nAssistant AG Shumate sent a memorandum to DOJ Civil Division employees on 6/11/25 setting Civil Division enforcement priorities, which include litigation against sanctuary jurisdictions and denaturalization, among others. The memo outlines categories of priorities for denaturalization cases.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"2a2521bb-4f85-4e24-9221-bd7dc1b52dcd","tool_use_result":{"query":"denaturalization task force USCIS cases pending legal challenges","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_019grjBNcRAL4rbJXeVB5sHr","content":[{"title":"Denaturalization Task Force How Citizenship Can Be Revoked","url":"https://www.shusterman.com/denaturalization/"},{"title":"Featured Issue: Denaturalization","url":"https://www.aila.org/featured-issue-denaturalization"},{"title":"FAQs: How Denaturalization Works | Immigrant Legal Resource Center | ILRC","url":"https://www.ilrc.org/community-resources/faqs-how-denaturalization-works"},{"title":"Featured Issue: Denaturalization Efforts by USCIS","url":"https://www.aila.org/library/featured-issue-denaturalization-efforts-by-uscis"},{"title":"Office of Public Affairs | The Department of Justice Creates Section Dedicated to Denaturalization Cases | United States Department of Justice","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/department-justice-creates-section-dedicated-denaturalization-cases"},{"title":"Office of the Assistant Attorney General U.S. Department of Justice","url":"https://www.justice.gov/civil/media/1404046/dl?inline="},{"title":"Denaturalization Under the Trump Administration: Legal Standards, Constitutional Constraints, and DOJ Enforcement Priorities - Law Offices of Michael D. BakerLaw Offices of Michael D. Baker","url":"https://mikebakerlaw.com/blog/2025/07/02/denaturalization-trump-administration-legal-standards-constitutional-constraints-doj-enforcement/"},{"title":"USCIS partners with Justice Department and Secures First Denaturalization As a Result of Operation Janus | USCIS","url":"https://www.uscis.gov/archive/uscis-partners-with-justice-department-and-secures-first-denaturalization-as-a-result-of-operation"},{"title":"Reported: USCIS ordered to escalate denaturalization case referrals | Immigration Policy Tracking Project","url":"https://immpolicytracking.org/policies/uscis-reportedly-set-to-escalate-denaturalization-case-referrals/"},{"title":"Can Trump Take Away My Citizenship: Denaturalization in 2025","url":"https://www.lawfirm4immigrants.com/can-trump-take-away-my-citizenship/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about the denaturalization task force, USCIS cases, and legal challenges:\n\n## USCIS Denaturalization Task Force and Case Volume\n\nIn December 2025, USCIS asked its field offices to \"supply Office of Immigration Litigation with 100-200 denaturalization cases per month\". By comparison, just over 120 cases have been filed between 2017 and 2025; this year, the DOJ has brought 13 denaturalization cases and won 8.\n\nThe USCIS has established an office in Los Angeles to focus on denaturalization. It will refer potential cases to the US Department of Justice.\n\n## Pending Cases and Legal Challenges\n\nSeveral legal and constitutional challenges exist to denaturalization efforts:\n\nIn Maslenjak v. United States (2017), the Supreme Court unanimously ruled that a naturalized citizen cannot lose their citizenship for a lie or omission unless the government proves that the falsehood was \"material\".\n\nAt this point in time, revocation of naturalization can only occur in federal courts. USCIS can only cancel the certification of naturalization, but cannot revoke the underlying status.\n\nThe 2025 Executive Order 14160 sought to redefine birthright citizenship, but it cannot retroactively revoke citizenship already granted under the 14th Amendment. The order's implementation is being challenged in court, and federal judges have blocked its application pending review.\n\nAssistant AG Shumate sent a memorandum to DOJ Civil Division employees on 6/11/25 setting Civil Division enforcement priorities, which include litigation against sanctuary jurisdictions and denaturalization, among others. The memo outlines categories of priorities for denaturalization cases."],"durationSeconds":8.638587800000002}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:04.675Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KwVRxSKH6BjyW9ZEHVMbVN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Maslenjak v United States denaturalization Supreme Court precedent\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Maslenjak v. United States - Harvard Law Review\",\"url\":\"https://harvardlawreview.org/print/vol-131/maslenjak-v-united-states/\"},{\"title\":\"16-309 Maslenjak v. United States (06/22/2017)\",\"url\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/16pdf/16-309_h31i.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Maslenjak v. United States | Supreme Court Bulletin | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/cert/16-309\"},{\"title\":\"Maslenjak v. United States - Ballotpedia\",\"url\":\"https://ballotpedia.org/Maslenjak_v._United_States\"},{\"title\":\"What the US Supreme Court Has Said About Denaturalization - Newsweek\",\"url\":\"https://www.newsweek.com/us-supreme-court-denaturalization-11135590\"},{\"title\":\"Academic highlight: Weil on denaturalization and the Supreme Court - SCOTUSblog\",\"url\":\"https://www.scotusblog.com/2017/06/academic-highlight-weil-denaturalization-supreme-court/\"},{\"title\":\"False Statements to Immigration Officials — Maslenjak v. ...\",\"url\":\"https://harvardlawreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/343-352_Online.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Maslenjak v. United States: False Statement Must be Material In Order to Lead to Denaturalization - MyAttorney USA\",\"url\":\"https://myattorneyusa.com/immigration-blog/citizenship/maslenjak-v-united-states-false-statement-must-be-material-in-order-to-lead-to-denaturalization/\"},{\"title\":\"MASLENJAK v. UNITED STATES | Supreme Court | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/16-309\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding Cases of Denaturalization\",\"url\":\"https://www.bajokalaw.com/understanding-cases-of-denaturalization\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what you need to know about *Maslenjak v. United States* as a denaturalization Supreme Court precedent:\n\n## Key Ruling\n\nThe Supreme Court held that in order to denaturalize a U.S. citizen under federal law for illegally procuring citizenship through false statements, the government must prove that those false statements were material to the decision to award citizenship. On June 22, 2017, in an opinion by Justice Elena Kagan, the court vacated and remanded the judgment of the Sixth Circuit.\n\n## The Precedent's Significance\n\nThe Supreme Court resolved a circuit split, holding that if the underlying illegal act is a false statement to government officials, the government must show that the falsehood influenced the decision to grant citizenship.\n\nThe Court insisted that \"the Government must establish that the defendant's illegal act played some role in her acquisition of citizenship\" and warned that it was \"unconvinced that Congress meant to grant prosecutors nearly limitless leverage over naturalized citizens.\"\n\n## Factual Background\n\nDivna Maslenjak and her family immigrated to the United States as refugees in the aftermath of the Bosnian War, claiming they feared persecution because Maslenjak's husband had avoided military conscription during the war. Maslenjak became a United States citizen in 2009, indicating on her application that she had never lied to immigration officials. United States officials discovered that Maslenjak's husband served as an officer in the Serbian Bratunac Brigade at the time the unit committed war crimes in the Bosnian War.\n\n## Broader Constitutional Impact\n\nThe limits set over the course of nearly a century reflect a consistent judicial view that denaturalization is a narrow tool reserved for cases of proven fraud, not a mechanism for political punishment or broad immigration enforcement.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"de39c4b5-dc51-4b87-8723-f4c1345f8847","tool_use_result":{"query":"Maslenjak v United States denaturalization Supreme Court precedent","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Kd254oCLWHRvcs3ECZBLUG","content":[{"title":"Maslenjak v. United States - Harvard Law Review","url":"https://harvardlawreview.org/print/vol-131/maslenjak-v-united-states/"},{"title":"16-309 Maslenjak v. United States (06/22/2017)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/16pdf/16-309_h31i.pdf"},{"title":"Maslenjak v. United States | Supreme Court Bulletin | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/cert/16-309"},{"title":"Maslenjak v. United States - Ballotpedia","url":"https://ballotpedia.org/Maslenjak_v._United_States"},{"title":"What the US Supreme Court Has Said About Denaturalization - Newsweek","url":"https://www.newsweek.com/us-supreme-court-denaturalization-11135590"},{"title":"Academic highlight: Weil on denaturalization and the Supreme Court - SCOTUSblog","url":"https://www.scotusblog.com/2017/06/academic-highlight-weil-denaturalization-supreme-court/"},{"title":"False Statements to Immigration Officials — Maslenjak v. ...","url":"https://harvardlawreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/343-352_Online.pdf"},{"title":"Maslenjak v. United States: False Statement Must be Material In Order to Lead to Denaturalization - MyAttorney USA","url":"https://myattorneyusa.com/immigration-blog/citizenship/maslenjak-v-united-states-false-statement-must-be-material-in-order-to-lead-to-denaturalization/"},{"title":"MASLENJAK v. UNITED STATES | Supreme Court | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/16-309"},{"title":"Understanding Cases of Denaturalization","url":"https://www.bajokalaw.com/understanding-cases-of-denaturalization"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what you need to know about *Maslenjak v. United States* as a denaturalization Supreme Court precedent:\n\n## Key Ruling\n\nThe Supreme Court held that in order to denaturalize a U.S. citizen under federal law for illegally procuring citizenship through false statements, the government must prove that those false statements were material to the decision to award citizenship. On June 22, 2017, in an opinion by Justice Elena Kagan, the court vacated and remanded the judgment of the Sixth Circuit.\n\n## The Precedent's Significance\n\nThe Supreme Court resolved a circuit split, holding that if the underlying illegal act is a false statement to government officials, the government must show that the falsehood influenced the decision to grant citizenship.\n\nThe Court insisted that \"the Government must establish that the defendant's illegal act played some role in her acquisition of citizenship\" and warned that it was \"unconvinced that Congress meant to grant prosecutors nearly limitless leverage over naturalized citizens.\"\n\n## Factual Background\n\nDivna Maslenjak and her family immigrated to the United States as refugees in the aftermath of the Bosnian War, claiming they feared persecution because Maslenjak's husband had avoided military conscription during the war. Maslenjak became a United States citizen in 2009, indicating on her application that she had never lied to immigration officials. United States officials discovered that Maslenjak's husband served as an officer in the Serbian Bratunac Brigade at the time the unit committed war crimes in the Bosnian War.\n\n## Broader Constitutional Impact\n\nThe limits set over the course of nearly a century reflect a consistent judicial view that denaturalization is a narrow tool reserved for cases of proven fraud, not a mechanism for political punishment or broad immigration enforcement."],"durationSeconds":8.4153944}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:05.514Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TNnp3qeJ3gY3xzaiADYyDe","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"immigration detention capacity 2025 Vera Institute deportation ratio\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ICE Detention Trends | Vera Institute of Justice\",\"url\":\"https://www.vera.org/ice-detention-trends\"},{\"title\":\"New Record: ICE Detainee Population Reaches High of 66,000\",\"url\":\"https://www.lawfirm4immigrants.com/ice-detainee-population-record-2025/\"},{\"title\":\"More People Are in Immigration Detention Than Ever Before | Vera Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.vera.org/news/more-people-are-in-immigration-detention-than-ever-before\"},{\"title\":\"Fact Check: How do ICE deportation statistics in 2025 compare to p...\",\"url\":\"https://factually.co/fact-checks/justice/ice-deportation-statistics-2025-vs-previous-years-3ded06\"},{\"title\":\"ICE Detainees Statistics in US 2026 | Key Facts - The World Data\",\"url\":\"https://theworlddata.com/ice-detainees-statistics-in-us/\"},{\"title\":\"The Truth About Immigration Detention in the United States | Vera Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.vera.org/news/the-truth-about-immigration-detention-in-the-united-states\"},{\"title\":\"Immigration Detention Legal Representation Crisis 2025\",\"url\":\"https://federalcriminaldefenseattorney.com/immigration-detention-legal-representation/\"},{\"title\":\"Immigration Detention Expansion in Trump’s Second Term - American Immigration Council\",\"url\":\"https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/report/immigration-detention/\"},{\"title\":\"Weaponizing the System: One Year of Trump’s Attacks on Due Process | Vera Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.vera.org/explainers/weaponizing-the-system-one-year-of-trumps-attacks-on-due-process\"},{\"title\":\"Detention of Immigrants | Vera Institute\",\"url\":\"https://www.vera.org/ending-mass-incarceration/reducing-incarceration/detention-of-immigrants\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding immigration detention capacity, the Vera Institute research, and deportation ratios in 2025:\n\n## Detention Capacity\n\nAs of December 2025, Vera's ICE Detention Trends tool uses data through October 15, 2025. When President Trump took office in January 2025, there were roughly 40,000 people being held in immigration detention, and by the start of December, that number had risen by almost 75 percent, with nearly 66,000 people held in immigration detention. The system is reportedly capable of holding 70,000 people on any given day — the highest level in history.\n\nFY 2025 appropriations totaled $4.3 billion for 45,000+ beds according to the Vera Institute. The Trump administration originally hoped to have nearly 108,000 immigration detention beds online by January 2026, though this ambitious goal has not been met.\n\n## Detention Facilities\n\nIn September 2025—the last full month covered by the data—ICE detained people in 528 facilities but acknowledged using just 189 of them on its website.\n\n## Deportation-to-Release Ratio\n\nAs of November 2025, for every one person released from ICE detention pending a hearing or after being granted relief, 14.3 people were deported directly from ICE custody, up from a ratio of 1.6 in December 2024. This dramatic shift reflects President Trump's January 20 order to maximize ICE's use of detention, after which ICE stopped issuing discretionary releases, and from January through November 29, discretionary releases from detention fell by 87 percent.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"668b3bfd-ad00-476f-bdad-24d26d3ddf6f","tool_use_result":{"query":"immigration detention capacity 2025 Vera Institute deportation ratio","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FZzBBre1cvimmjWpcMiiLU","content":[{"title":"ICE Detention Trends | Vera Institute of Justice","url":"https://www.vera.org/ice-detention-trends"},{"title":"New Record: ICE Detainee Population Reaches High of 66,000","url":"https://www.lawfirm4immigrants.com/ice-detainee-population-record-2025/"},{"title":"More People Are in Immigration Detention Than Ever Before | Vera Institute","url":"https://www.vera.org/news/more-people-are-in-immigration-detention-than-ever-before"},{"title":"Fact Check: How do ICE deportation statistics in 2025 compare to p...","url":"https://factually.co/fact-checks/justice/ice-deportation-statistics-2025-vs-previous-years-3ded06"},{"title":"ICE Detainees Statistics in US 2026 | Key Facts - The World Data","url":"https://theworlddata.com/ice-detainees-statistics-in-us/"},{"title":"The Truth About Immigration Detention in the United States | Vera Institute","url":"https://www.vera.org/news/the-truth-about-immigration-detention-in-the-united-states"},{"title":"Immigration Detention Legal Representation Crisis 2025","url":"https://federalcriminaldefenseattorney.com/immigration-detention-legal-representation/"},{"title":"Immigration Detention Expansion in Trump’s Second Term - American Immigration Council","url":"https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/report/immigration-detention/"},{"title":"Weaponizing the System: One Year of Trump’s Attacks on Due Process | Vera Institute","url":"https://www.vera.org/explainers/weaponizing-the-system-one-year-of-trumps-attacks-on-due-process"},{"title":"Detention of Immigrants | Vera Institute","url":"https://www.vera.org/ending-mass-incarceration/reducing-incarceration/detention-of-immigrants"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding immigration detention capacity, the Vera Institute research, and deportation ratios in 2025:\n\n## Detention Capacity\n\nAs of December 2025, Vera's ICE Detention Trends tool uses data through October 15, 2025. When President Trump took office in January 2025, there were roughly 40,000 people being held in immigration detention, and by the start of December, that number had risen by almost 75 percent, with nearly 66,000 people held in immigration detention. The system is reportedly capable of holding 70,000 people on any given day — the highest level in history.\n\nFY 2025 appropriations totaled $4.3 billion for 45,000+ beds according to the Vera Institute. The Trump administration originally hoped to have nearly 108,000 immigration detention beds online by January 2026, though this ambitious goal has not been met.\n\n## Detention Facilities\n\nIn September 2025—the last full month covered by the data—ICE detained people in 528 facilities but acknowledged using just 189 of them on its website.\n\n## Deportation-to-Release Ratio\n\nAs of November 2025, for every one person released from ICE detention pending a hearing or after being granted relief, 14.3 people were deported directly from ICE custody, up from a ratio of 1.6 in December 2024. This dramatic shift reflects President Trump's January 20 order to maximize ICE's use of detention, after which ICE stopped issuing discretionary releases, and from January through November 29, discretionary releases from detention fell by 87 percent."],"durationSeconds":8.550457900000001}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:05.975Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EuaUhF1z5WcgJCvwppmg78","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"denaturalization proceedings burden of proof standard judicial review\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Denaturalization Under the Trump Administration: Legal Standards, Constitutional Constraints, and DOJ Enforcement Priorities - Law Offices of Michael D. BakerLaw Offices of Michael D. Baker\",\"url\":\"https://mikebakerlaw.com/blog/2025/07/02/denaturalization-trump-administration-legal-standards-constitutional-constraints-doj-enforcement/\"},{\"title\":\"Woodby v. INS | 385 U.S. 276 (1966) | Justia U.S. Supreme Court Center\",\"url\":\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/385/276/\"},{\"title\":\"Clear, Unequivocal, and Convincing Evidence Standard in Deportation Proceedings Established in Woodby v. INS: U.S. | CaseMine\",\"url\":\"https://www.casemine.com/commentary/us/clear,-unequivocal,-and-convincing-evidence-standard-in-deportation-proceedings-established-in-woodby-v.-ins/view\"},{\"title\":\"The Denaturalization Consequences of Guilty Pleas | Yale Law Journal\",\"url\":\"https://yalelawjournal.org/forum/the-denaturalization-consequences-of-guilty-pleas\"},{\"title\":\"DENATURALIZATION AND REVOCATION OF ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ilrc.org/sites/default/files/resources/denaturalization_pa.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"REVOCATION OF CITIZENSHIP\",\"url\":\"https://biotech.law.lsu.edu/blaw/olc/ina340.htm\"},{\"title\":\"DENATURALIZATION\",\"url\":\"https://www.ilrc.org/sites/default/files/resources/denaturalization_und_prcs_rcnt_effrts-20181221.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Denaturalization in 2025: What You Need to Know About the Government Revoking Citizenship | ASIRILAW, PLLC\",\"url\":\"https://www.asirilaw.com/denaturalization-in-2025-what-you-need-to-know-about-the-government-revoking-citizenship\"},{\"title\":\"Denaturalization: Fact Sheet - National Immigration Forum\",\"url\":\"https://forumtogether.org/article/denaturalization-fact-sheet/\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 1- Purpose and Background | USCIS\",\"url\":\"https://www.uscis.gov/policy-manual/volume-12-part-l-chapter-1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here is information about the burden of proof standard and judicial review in denaturalization proceedings:\n\n## Burden of Proof Standard\n\nIn denaturalization cases, the Court has required the Government to establish its allegations by clear, unequivocal, and convincing evidence. The \"clear, convincing, and unequivocal evidence\" standard requires overwhelming proof, with courts interpreting ambiguities in favor of the citizen.\n\nThe Supreme Court held that the burden of proof for civil denaturalization proceedings is \"clear, unequivocal, and convincing\" evidence which does not leave \"the issue in doubt.\" This standard comes from the landmark Supreme Court case *Schneiderman v. United States*.\n\nThis burden is substantially identical to the criminal beyond reasonable doubt standard despite being civil proceedings. For criminal denaturalization cases specifically, the government satisfies the beyond a reasonable doubt standard in a comparable criminal case.\n\n## Judicial Review\n\nUnlike most other immigration proceedings that USCIS handles in an administrative setting, revocation of naturalization can only occur in federal court. For civil revocation of naturalization, the United States Attorney's Office must file the revocation of naturalization actions in Federal District Court. For criminal revocation of naturalization, the U.S. Attorney's Office files criminal charges in Federal District Court.\n\nThe Court determined that statutory provisions cited by the lower courts pertained to the scope of judicial review rather than the burden of proof at the administrative level.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"7c0f35d6-8604-4b81-947d-b09bd2970e92","tool_use_result":{"query":"denaturalization proceedings burden of proof standard judicial review","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01EteqePj6DUkd59WnShbesL","content":[{"title":"Denaturalization Under the Trump Administration: Legal Standards, Constitutional Constraints, and DOJ Enforcement Priorities - Law Offices of Michael D. BakerLaw Offices of Michael D. Baker","url":"https://mikebakerlaw.com/blog/2025/07/02/denaturalization-trump-administration-legal-standards-constitutional-constraints-doj-enforcement/"},{"title":"Woodby v. INS | 385 U.S. 276 (1966) | Justia U.S. Supreme Court Center","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/385/276/"},{"title":"Clear, Unequivocal, and Convincing Evidence Standard in Deportation Proceedings Established in Woodby v. INS: U.S. | CaseMine","url":"https://www.casemine.com/commentary/us/clear,-unequivocal,-and-convincing-evidence-standard-in-deportation-proceedings-established-in-woodby-v.-ins/view"},{"title":"The Denaturalization Consequences of Guilty Pleas | Yale Law Journal","url":"https://yalelawjournal.org/forum/the-denaturalization-consequences-of-guilty-pleas"},{"title":"DENATURALIZATION AND REVOCATION OF ...","url":"https://www.ilrc.org/sites/default/files/resources/denaturalization_pa.pdf"},{"title":"REVOCATION OF CITIZENSHIP","url":"https://biotech.law.lsu.edu/blaw/olc/ina340.htm"},{"title":"DENATURALIZATION","url":"https://www.ilrc.org/sites/default/files/resources/denaturalization_und_prcs_rcnt_effrts-20181221.pdf"},{"title":"Denaturalization in 2025: What You Need to Know About the Government Revoking Citizenship | ASIRILAW, PLLC","url":"https://www.asirilaw.com/denaturalization-in-2025-what-you-need-to-know-about-the-government-revoking-citizenship"},{"title":"Denaturalization: Fact Sheet - National Immigration Forum","url":"https://forumtogether.org/article/denaturalization-fact-sheet/"},{"title":"Chapter 1- Purpose and Background | USCIS","url":"https://www.uscis.gov/policy-manual/volume-12-part-l-chapter-1"}]},"Based on the search results, here is information about the burden of proof standard and judicial review in denaturalization proceedings:\n\n## Burden of Proof Standard\n\nIn denaturalization cases, the Court has required the Government to establish its allegations by clear, unequivocal, and convincing evidence. The \"clear, convincing, and unequivocal evidence\" standard requires overwhelming proof, with courts interpreting ambiguities in favor of the citizen.\n\nThe Supreme Court held that the burden of proof for civil denaturalization proceedings is \"clear, unequivocal, and convincing\" evidence which does not leave \"the issue in doubt.\" This standard comes from the landmark Supreme Court case *Schneiderman v. United States*.\n\nThis burden is substantially identical to the criminal beyond reasonable doubt standard despite being civil proceedings. For criminal denaturalization cases specifically, the government satisfies the beyond a reasonable doubt standard in a comparable criminal case.\n\n## Judicial Review\n\nUnlike most other immigration proceedings that USCIS handles in an administrative setting, revocation of naturalization can only occur in federal court. For civil revocation of naturalization, the United States Attorney's Office must file the revocation of naturalization actions in Federal District Court. For criminal revocation of naturalization, the U.S. Attorney's Office files criminal charges in Federal District Court.\n\nThe Court determined that statutory provisions cited by the lower courts pertained to the scope of judicial review rather than the burden of proof at the administrative level."],"durationSeconds":8.426214400000001}}
