ality), I would need either:\n\n1. A properly formatted text version of the case\n2. A readable PDF that displays the opinion content\n3. A citation to access the case through standard legal databases\n\n**What I can share generally:** Maslenjak addressed whether the government must prove fraud in the naturalization process was \"material\" to establish grounds for denaturalization. The Court established important due process protections regarding the standard of proof required.\n\nIf you can provide the case text in a readable format, I'd be happy to extract the specific holdings regarding materiality requirements and procedural protections while adhering to your character limits and quotation guidelines.","durationMs":4938,"url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/16pdf/16-309_h31i.pdf"}}
[2026-02-04T17:38:19.867Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011QL2DDNM157zDpJ7NW1E5m","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary of 8 U.S.C. § 1451: Revocation of Naturalization\n\n## Grounds for Denaturalization\n\nThe statute establishes several bases for revoking citizenship:\n\n**Illegal Procurement or Fraud (Subsection a):** The government may revoke naturalization where \"the order and certificate of naturalization were illegally procured or were procured by concealment of a material fact or by willful misrepresentation.\"\n\n**Refusal to Testify:** Within ten years of naturalization, refusal to testify before congressional committees regarding subversive activities—when resulting in contempt conviction—constitutes grounds for revocation.\n\n**Membership in Prohibited Organizations (Subsection c):** For persons naturalized after December 24, 1952, joining organizations that would have precluded naturalization within five years creates \"prima facie evidence\" supporting revocation.\n\n**Criminal Conviction (Subsection e):** Conviction under 18 U.S.C. § 1425 for knowingly procuring naturalization unlawfully triggers automatic revocation by the trial court.\n\n## Procedural Requirements\n\n**Notice and Opportunity to Respond:** The defendant receives \"sixty days' personal notice\" to answer allegations, with alternative service methods available for absent persons.\n\n**Jurisdiction:** Cases may be filed where the naturalized citizen resides, last resided, or in the District of Columbia.\n\n**Court Authority:** The court must cancel certificates and send certified copies to the Attorney General.\n\n## Time Limitations\n\n- **General fraud claims:** No specific statutory limitation period\n- **Organization membership:** Five-year window following naturalization\n- **Refusal to testify:** Ten-year window post-naturalization\n- **Derivative citizenship loss:** Applies regardless of current residence"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"dbdf3ff3-eeb4-4664-89dd-52b941eda1b4","tool_use_result":{"bytes":67274,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Summary of 8 U.S.C. § 1451: Revocation of Naturalization\n\n## Grounds for Denaturalization\n\nThe statute establishes several bases for revoking citizenship:\n\n**Illegal Procurement or Fraud (Subsection a):** The government may revoke naturalization where \"the order and certificate of naturalization were illegally procured or were procured by concealment of a material fact or by willful misrepresentation.\"\n\n**Refusal to Testify:** Within ten years of naturalization, refusal to testify before congressional committees regarding subversive activities—when resulting in contempt conviction—constitutes grounds for revocation.\n\n**Membership in Prohibited Organizations (Subsection c):** For persons naturalized after December 24, 1952, joining organizations that would have precluded naturalization within five years creates \"prima facie evidence\" supporting revocation.\n\n**Criminal Conviction (Subsection e):** Conviction under 18 U.S.C. § 1425 for knowingly procuring naturalization unlawfully triggers automatic revocation by the trial court.\n\n## Procedural Requirements\n\n**Notice and Opportunity to Respond:** The defendant receives \"sixty days' personal notice\" to answer allegations, with alternative service methods available for absent persons.\n\n**Jurisdiction:** Cases may be filed where the naturalized citizen resides, last resided, or in the District of Columbia.\n\n**Court Authority:** The court must cancel certificates and send certified copies to the Attorney General.\n\n## Time Limitations\n\n- **General fraud claims:** No specific statutory limitation period\n- **Organization membership:** Five-year window following naturalization\n- **Refusal to testify:** Ten-year window post-naturalization\n- **Derivative citizenship loss:** Applies regardless of current residence","durationMs":7072,"url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/8/1451"}}
